<Page title="Now · Serina McFall" description="What's active right now, what comes next, what's still on the horizon.">
  <article class="now">
    <header class="opener">
      <h1>Now, next, and later.</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated <time datetime={lastUpdated}>{lastUpdated}</time></p>
    </header>

        <section>
      <h2>Now</h2>
      <p>I am on industry placement at Westpac New Zealand as a Forward Deployment Engineering Intern in Digital Tech, through to 21 December. The work is system analysis: teams have internal tools and software nobody has fully documented, and I go in to work out what they actually do and map them, so the people who rely on them can decide what needs to happen next. Three business units so far — Voice of Customer, Westpac Business, and Customer Services, which I am on now.</p>
      <p>Dev Academy's AI Launchpad finished on 13 November, assessed under the New Zealand Certificate in Applied Digital Technology, Level 6 — completed, with results pending. The group project was on our cohort's fork of Buzz, an open-source hive-mind communication platform, where I owned the documentation corpus: 233 files covering architecture, schema, standards and capabilities. The corpus stays in our fork rather than going upstream.</p>
      <p>Out of that project came three tools. The Professor, a skill that creates docs, maintains them and vets them in one pass, because docs are my least favourite job and I would rather automate the whole loop than the first draft. A review gate. And a test automation suite that has since outgrown the project entirely. I am finishing all three for myself now.</p>
      <p>Alongside that, Caladryn, my Next.js worldbuilding site, where the accessibility rules are enforced in the build rather than left to memory.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>Looking for my next role, available from 22 December — Auckland or remote, and server-side leaning.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Later</h2>
      <p>The Learning Companion Project, an AI-assisted study tool for neurodivergent brains, built on the agent and worker patterns I have spent this year learning. And the role this site is meant to find me.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Page>

